CAT = "sgsnbSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
Q = [
('Which is one of the WHO Five Moments for hand hygiene?',
 "After touching a patient's bed area", ["Before reading the patient's chart", 'After answering the ward telephone', 'Before entering the ward at the start of shift'],
 "The five moments are before touching a patient, before a clean or aseptic procedure, after body fluid exposure risk, after touching a patient and after touching a patient's surroundings."),
('When are soap and water preferred to an alcohol-based hand rub?',
 'When hands are visibly soiled', ['Before every patient contact', 'When gloves have been worn', 'After using the computer'],
 'Alcohol rub is used when hands are visibly clean; visible soiling, or caring for a patient with Clostridioides difficile or norovirus diarrhoea, calls for soap and water.'),
('A nurse must remove PPE after caring for a patient on contact precautions. Which item is removed first?',
 'Gloves', ['The mask or respirator', 'The eye protection', 'The hair cover'],
 'Gloves are the most contaminated item and come off first, followed by hand hygiene, gown, eye protection and mask, with hand hygiene again at the end.'),
('Which type of precautions applies to a patient with active pulmonary tuberculosis?',
 'Airborne, in a negative pressure room', ['Contact precautions in a single room', 'Droplet precautions with a surgical mask', 'Standard precautions only for all contacts'],
 'TB spreads through small airborne particles; the patient is placed in an airborne infection isolation room and staff wear a fit-tested N95 respirator.'),
('A patient has influenza. Which precaution is added to standard precautions?',
 'Droplet, with a surgical mask', ['Airborne, with an N95 respirator only', 'Contact precautions with a gown only', 'Reverse isolation in a positive pressure room'],
 'Influenza spreads mainly by large droplets over short distances, so staff wear a surgical mask within about a metre of the patient.'),
('After a needlestick injury, what should the nurse do first?',
 'Wash with soap and running water', ['Squeeze the wound hard to bleed it out', 'Scrub with a brush and bleach', 'Cover with a dressing and carry on working'],
 'The wound is washed with soap and running water and not scrubbed or squeezed. It is then reported and assessed for post-exposure treatment.'),
('Where must a used needle be placed?',
 'Directly into a sharps container', ['Back into its cap with both hands', 'In the clinical waste bag', 'On a tray for later disposal'],
 'Sharps go straight into a puncture-resistant container at the point of use without recapping, bending or breaking.'),
('A nurse is setting up a sterile field. What indicates the field is contaminated?',
 'Touching it with a wet, non-sterile item', ['A sterile glove touching the inside edge', 'Placing sterile items inside the field', 'Opening a sterile pack away from the body'],
 'Moisture lets microbes travel through sterile material, and anything non-sterile touching the field contaminates it. The 2.5 cm border is also considered non-sterile.'),
('Which patient is at highest risk for a pressure injury?',
 'An immobile, malnourished older adult', ['A young adult after minor dental surgery', 'An ambulant patient with a mild cold', 'A well-nourished patient who jogs daily'],
 'Immobility, poor nutrition, moisture and reduced sensation raise pressure injury risk, so assessment tools like the Braden scale are used.'),
('Which intervention best prevents pressure injuries in a bedbound patient?',
 'Reposition at least every two hours', ['Massage reddened bony areas', 'Use a ring cushion', 'Keep the head of the bed flat'],
 'Regular repositioning relieves pressure; massaging damaged skin and ring cushions can increase tissue damage.'),
('A patient with dementia keeps trying to climb out of bed. What is the first approach?',
 'Look for causes and try alternatives', ['Apply wrist restraints at once', 'Sedate the patient routinely', 'Leave the rails down and observe from afar'],
 'Restraints are the last resort. Nurses first look for causes such as pain, toileting or boredom and use alternatives like a low bed and closer observation.'),
('Which action reduces the risk of falls in an older inpatient?',
 'Keep the call bell and aid in reach', ['Keep the bed at its highest level', 'Place the patient in a dim, quiet room', 'Raise bed rails to stop him from leaving'],
 'Items within reach, a low bed, adequate lighting, non-slip footwear and regular toileting reduce falls; raised rails may increase injury from climbing over.'),
('Which is the correct way for a nurse to lift a patient up in bed?',
 'Use a slide sheet with two staff', ["Pull under the patient's arms alone", 'Bend at the waist and lift quickly', 'Ask the patient to lift himself fully'],
 'Safe handling uses aids and assistance and good body mechanics to protect both the patient and the staff.'),
('Chlorhexidine skin preparation is used before inserting an IV cannula. What is the correct application?',
 'Apply with friction and allow to dry', ['Wipe once and place the cannula while wet', 'Apply it and wipe it off at once', 'Apply it after the cannula is in place'],
 'Skin antisepsis needs friction and adequate contact time to dry before puncture; wet antiseptic may not have killed the organisms.'),
('Which is an appropriate way to prevent catheter-associated urinary tract infection?',
 'Remove it as soon as it is not needed', ['Disconnect the bag to empty it often', 'Change the catheter every week as routine', 'Irrigate the bladder daily with antibiotics'],
 'The most effective prevention is removing the catheter when no longer indicated and keeping the system closed, with the bag below bladder level.'),
('A patient is incontinent of stool. Which care best protects the skin?',
 'Clean promptly, apply a barrier cream', ['Leave the area moist to soften the skin', 'Rub firmly with a dry rough cloth', 'Use heavily perfumed soaps each time'],
 'Prompt gentle cleansing, pat drying and a barrier cream reduce moisture-associated skin damage.'),
("Where should a nurse position an oxygen cylinder in a patient's room?",
 'Secured upright, away from heat', ['Lying flat under the bed', 'Near the heater to keep it warm', 'Hanging from the bed rail by a strap'],
 'Cylinders are secured upright away from ignition sources since oxygen supports combustion, and smoking and open flames are not allowed near it.'),
("A fire starts in a patient's room. Which action fits RACE?",
 'Rescue people in immediate danger', ['Fight the fire with water first', 'Open all the doors to ventilate', "Return the patient's meal tray"],
 'RACE stands for rescue, alarm, contain and extinguish or evacuate, in that order.'),
('What should a nurse do before giving care to a patient on a bariatric bed?',
 "Check the equipment's safe working load", ['Use ordinary equipment to save time', 'Lift without help if at all possible', 'Ask the patient to stay perfectly still'],
 'Beds, hoists and chairs have a safe working load; using inadequate equipment risks injury to patient and nurse.'),
('A patient on bed rest develops calf swelling and pain. Which condition is most suspected?',
 'Deep vein thrombosis', ['Pressure injury', 'Muscle spasm only', 'Urinary tract infection'],
 'Immobility increases clot risk; unilateral calf pain, swelling and warmth suggest DVT, which the nurse reports urgently without massaging the leg.'),
]
